fuse: skip the capability lookup before a write when the server kills privileges

Before the first write to a regular file, file_remove_privs() asks whether the file carries security.capability,
which on FUSE is a GETXATTR round trip to the server (on virtiofs here about 60 us, synchronous in the write path).
The answer is never acted on when the server negotiated FUSE_HANDLE_KILLPRIV_V2: the kernel already marks every
unprivileged write with FUSE_WRITE_KILL_SUIDGID and leaves killing suid, sgid and the capability to the server. The
VFS caches a negative answer in S_NOSEC, but fuse_change_attributes_common() clears that flag on every attribute
refresh (another client might have set a capability), and a created file's attributes are refreshed at once, so every
file paid once: an npm ci of 34,500 files into a bind mount sent 34,900 such requests.

Mark a regular file's inode S_NOSEC when it is created and keep the flag across attribute refreshes when the server
handles privileges (inode_has_no_xattr() sets it under SB_NOSEC, which such a connection sets, and only for a file
without suid or sgid bits). The VFS still clears S_NOSEC when a chmod gives the file those bits, and a write to a file
that gained them on the server's side is still stripped by the server.

Signed-off-by: smolvm <noreply@smolvm>
---
diff --git a/fs/fuse/inode.c b/fs/fuse/inode.c
--- a/fs/fuse/inode.c
+++ b/fs/fuse/inode.c
@@ -311,8 +311,14 @@
 	 * was set or if security.capability xattr was set. But we don't
 	 * know if security.capability has been set or not. So clear it
 	 * anyway. Its less efficient but should be safe.
+	 *
+	 * Unless the server handles privileges (FUSE_HANDLE_KILLPRIV_V2):
+	 * it strips suid, sgid and the capability on every write it is
+	 * told to, whatever another client set, so the flag can stay and
+	 * the first write to each file is spared a GETXATTR.
 	 */
-	inode->i_flags &= ~S_NOSEC;
+	if (!fc->handle_killpriv_v2)
+		inode->i_flags &= ~S_NOSEC;
 }
 
 u32 fuse_get_cache_mask(struct inode *inode)
@@ -511,6 +517,14 @@
 			inode->i_flags |= S_NOCMTIME;
 		inode->i_generation = generation;
 		fuse_init_inode(inode, attr, fc);
+		/*
+		 * With FUSE_HANDLE_KILLPRIV_V2 the server strips suid, sgid
+		 * and security.capability on write itself, so the VFS need
+		 * not ask for the capability before the first write to the
+		 * file (a round trip per file on virtiofs).
+		 */
+		if (fc->handle_killpriv_v2 && S_ISREG(inode->i_mode))
+			inode_has_no_xattr(inode);
 		unlock_new_inode(inode);
 	} else if (fuse_stale_inode(inode, generation, attr)) {
 		/* nodeid was reused, any I/O on the old inode should fail */
